\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RADJEMPTYSUBST1        (*subst of EMPTY as a prepobjrel/..*)
                               (* x1 is afraid, x1 is surprised, 
                               *)
                                  
< m1: I1::ADJPFORMULA{ADJPFORMULArec1}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/ADJP{ADJPrec1}
                      I8:[ I2:mu2,
                           prepobjrel/EMPTYVAR{EMPTYVARrec1},
                           I3:mu3
                         ]
                ]
SUBST:
  m2: EMPTY(KEY_zeroemptykey){EMPTYrec1}            (*substituent*)
>

< m : I9::ADJPFORMULA{ADJPFORMULArec2}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/ADJP{ADJPrec2}
                      I8:[ I2:mu2,
                           I6:mu3
                         ]
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: ADJPFORMULArec1.adjpatternefs * [synPREPEMPTY] <> []
       I2: ALL(mu2, I4:rel1/..)
       I3: NOT EXIST(mu3, [prepobjrel/..])
       I4: rel1 IN [negrel, causadvrel, locadvrel, tempadvrel, degreemodrel,
                    head] 
       I6: NOT EXIST(mu3, [prepobjrel/..]) AND
           ( (NOT EXIST(mu3,[rel10/EMPTYVAR{}]) ) AND
             (NOT EXIST(mu3,[rel11/VARPREPP{}[I7:mu5]]))
           )   (* substordercond *)
       I7: EXIST(mu5,[../EMPTYVAR{}])
       I8: RIGHTTOLEFT
       I9: ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs * [synPREPEMPTY] <> []
      >


   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >


      COMP
         <
          C1: EMPTYVARrec1.index = LEVEL
          A1: ADJPFORMULArec2 := ADJPFORMULArec1;
              ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs := 
                 ADJPFORMULArec1.adjpatternefs * [synPREPEMPTY];
              ADJPrec2 := ADJPrec1;
              ADJPrec2.adjpatternefs := 
                 ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs 
         >
      DECOMP
         < 
          C1:  ADJPrec2.adjpatternefs = ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs 
          A1: EMPTYVARrec1.index := LEVEL;
              ADJPrec1 := ADJPrec2;
              ADJPFORMULArec1 := ADJPFORMULArec2;
              ADJPFORMULArec1.adjpatternefs := 
                 ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs * [synPREPEMPTY];
              ADJPrec1.adjpatternefs := 
                 ADJPFORMULArec1.adjpatternefs

         >

&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RADJEMPTYSUBST1
\item[* relevant examples:] x1 afraid of EMPTY, x1 surprised about EMPTY
\item[* language:]  English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] ADJPPROPTOADJPFORMULA
\item[* class:] RC:EMPTYSUBST
\item[* date of creation:] 27-MAY-1988 10:37:06  
\item[* author:] Franciska de Jong
                 
\item[* partly copied from:] 
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To substitute EMPTYs as prepobjrel/..
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
In analysis there is to be added 
a check on the substitution order, which does not exist
in generation.
The check does not exist in generation because the relevant languages 
may differ w.r.t order of substitution, though this difference in substitution
never has any consequences for the meaning. 
In analysis a check on substitution order is made to avoid superfluous 
ambiguities, i.e. to avoid many derivations to which only one meaning 
corresponds. 
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] Existential Quantification
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}This rule should should not apply to cases
such as the dutch adj {\em geschikt}, that do not involve Existential 
Quantification. It is still to be established how
these cases should be dealt with. Two possibilities: 
\begin{enumerate} 
\item by means of DISCEMPTYsubstitution,  thus accounting for the
fact that the second argument of the adj is to be found in the context of
discourse. Cf. also RADJEMPTYsubstitution4 for EMPTY's with locargrel.

\item as an idiom corresponding to for example `fit for it'.

\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
