\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RADJEMPTYSUBST2          (* TOEMPTY of FOREMPTY ????? *)
                               (*subst of EMPTY as a indobjrel/..*)
                               (* she is important;
                                  she is known
                               *)
                                  
< m1: I1::ADJPFORMULA{ADJPFORMULArec1}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/ADJP{ADJPrec1}
                      I8:[ I2:mu2,
                           indobjrel/EMPTYVAR{EMPTYVARrec1},
                           I3:mu3
                         ],
                  mu4 
                ]
SUBST:
  m2: EMPTY(KEY_zeroemptykey){EMPTYrec1}            (*substituent*)
>

< m : I9::ADJPFORMULA{ADJPFORMULArec2}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/ADJP{ADJPrec2}
                      I8:[ I2:mu2,
                           I6:mu3
                         ],
                  mu4 
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: ADJPFORMULArec1.adjpatternefs * [synFOREMPTY] <> []
       I2: ALL(mu2, I4:rel1/..) 
       I3: NOT EXIST(mu3, [indobjrel/..])
       I4: rel1 IN [negrel, causadvrel, locadvrel, tempadvrel, head,
                    degreemodrel] 
       I6: NOT EXIST(mu3, [indobjrel/..]) AND
           ( (NOT EXIST(mu3,[rel10/EMPTYVAR{}]) ) AND
             (NOT EXIST(mu3,[rel11/VARPREPP{}[I7:mu5]]))
           )   (* substordercond *)
       I7: EXIST(mu5,[../EMPTYVAR{}])

       I8: RIGHTTOLEFT
       I9: ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs * [synFOREMPTY] <> []
      >

   PARAMETERS
      < 
       LEVEL
      >
      COMP
         <
          C1: EMPTYVARrec1.index = LEVEL
          A1: ADJPFORMULArec2 := ADJPFORMULArec1;
              ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs := 
                 ADJPFORMULArec1.adjpatternefs * [synFOREMPTY] ;
              ADJPrec2 := ADJPrec1;
              ADJPrec2.adjpatternefs := 
                 ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs 
         >
      DECOMP
         < 
          C1:  ADJPrec2.adjpatternefs = ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs 
          A1: EMPTYVARrec1.index := LEVEL;
              ADJPrec1 := ADJPrec2;
              ADJPFORMULArec1 := ADJPFORMULArec2;
              ADJPFORMULArec1.adjpatternefs := 
                 ADJPFORMULArec2.adjpatternefs * [synFOREMPTY];
              ADJPrec1.adjpatternefs := 
                 ADJPFORMULArec1.adjpatternefs
         >


&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RADJEMPTYSUBST2
\item[* relevant examples:] she is important, she is known
\item[* language:] English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] ADJPPROPTOADJPFORMULA
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class)
\item[* date of creation:] 29-SEP-1987 15:37:06  
\item[* author:] Franciska de Jong
                 
\item[* partly copied from:] Jan Odijk's REMPTYSubstitution2
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To substitute PREPEMPTYs as prepobjrel/..
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
In analysis there is a check on the substitution order, which does not exist
in generation.
The check does not exist in generation because the relevant languages 
may differ w.r.t order of substitution, though this difference in substitution
never has any consequences for the meaning. 
In analysis a check on substitution order is made to avoid superfluous 
ambiguities, i.e. to avoid many derivations to which only one meaning 
corresponds. 
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] NOT: Existential Quantification. Actually, the 
semantics of empty voor-objects is context-dependent and needs investigation.
In order to distinguish REMPTYSubst2 from REMPTYSubst1, it might be useful to
introduce two new empty categories: DISCEMPTY and DISCPREPEMPTY. 
Pending a discussion, this rule class presumes only EMPTY.
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{} At least some of the voor-objs 
are real arguments. If all voor-objs are to be treated as semi-objects (as
suggested on December 15, this rule might become superfluous.
